[{:db/id "ca4b7fd6-e628-5a99-898f-4ef27ca7dfc5" :answer/id #uuid "ca4b7fd6-e628-5a99-898f-4ef27ca7dfc5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3 \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "b35d874d-13e5-52c0-93cc-3d0c92d94055" :answer/id #uuid "b35d874d-13e5-52c0-93cc-3d0c92d94055" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3} \\pi}{4}" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "faaf8fd0-a189-5a52-a852-f75ff62ece6d" :answer/id #uuid "faaf8fd0-a189-5a52-a852-f75ff62ece6d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3} \\pi}{2}" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "9a7490fb-e639-56fb-bc3d-36e00d2fe872" :answer/id #uuid "9a7490fb-e639-56fb-bc3d-36e00d2fe872" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{3} \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "1cb0f77c-0203-5719-ab9d-91ce8ca040f7" :answer/id #uuid "1cb0f77c-0203-5719-ab9d-91ce8ca040f7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "4e19b400-81aa-5f00-9d55-be29eb1d866d" :answer-field/id #uuid "4e19b400-81aa-5f00-9d55-be29eb1d866d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["ca4b7fd6-e628-5a99-898f-4ef27ca7dfc5" "b35d874d-13e5-52c0-93cc-3d0c92d94055" "faaf8fd0-a189-5a52-a852-f75ff62ece6d" "9a7490fb-e639-56fb-bc3d-36e00d2fe872" "1cb0f77c-0203-5719-ab9d-91ce8ca040f7"] :answer-field/correct "9a7490fb-e639-56fb-bc3d-36e00d2fe872" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "873a8289-cea9-556f-b99b-1f305a18b7fd" :question/id #uuid "873a8289-cea9-556f-b99b-1f305a18b7fd" :question/problem "![](images/fb/fbbb7c260754e6579bedc6e773656ea8defdb0493117f05e0e5f02fab4afb36c.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\\iint_S 1\\,\\mathrm{d}S,$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=- x + y$ and whose domain is the disk of radius $1$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Give an exact answer." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\\iint_S f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(- x + y)=-1,$$\n\nand\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- x + y)=1.$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S{1}\\,\\mathrm{d}S & =\\iint_D{f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(1)\\cdot\\sqrt{1+(-1)^2+(1)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(1)\\cdot\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{1}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ our domain can be expressed as\n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 1,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le 2 \\pi\\}.$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{1}\\,\\mathrm{d}A & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{(1)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\int_{0}^{1}{r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\left[\\frac{r^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\left[((\\frac{1}{2}))-((0))\\right]}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\frac{1}{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})\\left[\\theta\\right]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})\\left[((2 \\pi))-((0))\\right] \\\\ & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})\\cdot((2 \\pi)-(0)) \\\\ & =\\sqrt{3} \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral is $\\sqrt{3} \\pi$." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["4e19b400-81aa-5f00-9d55-be29eb1d866d"] :db/ensure :question/validate} {:db/id "f7f0bb7a-9649-5c57-b6ec-ac42c2d42781" :answer/id #uuid "f7f0bb7a-9649-5c57-b6ec-ac42c2d42781" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4 \\sqrt{3} \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "77bfa40c-0afd-5cb8-aa5c-b34ba65cbdf6" :answer/id #uuid "77bfa40c-0afd-5cb8-aa5c-b34ba65cbdf6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16 \\sqrt{3} \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "d1731e24-69f6-5cbf-9e13-2f9603b51d48" :answer/id #uuid "d1731e24-69f6-5cbf-9e13-2f9603b51d48" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "48 \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "069dbf70-7416-5aa9-b93b-1c0d75ec550f" :answer/id #uuid "069dbf70-7416-5aa9-b93b-1c0d75ec550f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8 \\sqrt{3} \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "0319e11a-534e-5e66-aa6f-c12e5f170d43" :answer/id #uuid "0319e11a-534e-5e66-aa6f-c12e5f170d43" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16 \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "779704ff-9be7-5e93-805f-a309e7bb251f" :answer-field/id #uuid "779704ff-9be7-5e93-805f-a309e7bb251f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["f7f0bb7a-9649-5c57-b6ec-ac42c2d42781" "77bfa40c-0afd-5cb8-aa5c-b34ba65cbdf6" "d1731e24-69f6-5cbf-9e13-2f9603b51d48" "069dbf70-7416-5aa9-b93b-1c0d75ec550f" "0319e11a-534e-5e66-aa6f-c12e5f170d43"] :answer-field/correct "77bfa40c-0afd-5cb8-aa5c-b34ba65cbdf6" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "8aec1038-bbbf-5f1f-8cad-aecf57bf997f" :question/id #uuid "8aec1038-bbbf-5f1f-8cad-aecf57bf997f" :question/problem "![](images/73/73d9ffd3c1ab9ad243b1944fe53a2654f89418f34262e8fd44dcf0ef057e79e5.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\\iint_S 4\\,\\mathrm{d}S,$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=- x + y$ and whose domain is the disk of radius $2$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Give an exact answer." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\\iint_S f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(- x + y)=-1,$$\n\nand\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- x + y)=1.$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}S & =\\iint_D{f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(4)\\cdot\\sqrt{1+(-1)^2+(1)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(4)\\cdot\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ our domain can be expressed as\n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 2,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le 2 \\pi\\}.$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}A & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{(4)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{4 r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\int_{0}^{2}{4 r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\left[2 r^{2}\\right]_{0}^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\left[((8))-((0))\\right]}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{8}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(8 \\sqrt{3})\\left[\\theta\\right]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ & =(8 \\sqrt{3})\\left[((2 \\pi))-((0))\\right] \\\\ & =(8 \\sqrt{3})\\cdot((2 \\pi)-(0)) \\\\ & =16 \\sqrt{3} \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral is $16 \\sqrt{3} \\pi$." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["779704ff-9be7-5e93-805f-a309e7bb251f"] :db/ensure :question/validate} {:db/id "b570d8ce-b35e-58a9-a28f-8963dd0ebe8a" :answer/id #uuid "b570d8ce-b35e-58a9-a28f-8963dd0ebe8a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6 \\sqrt{3} \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "a55d1153-0d00-5bef-9de8-4db03c5e4271" :answer/id #uuid "a55d1153-0d00-5bef-9de8-4db03c5e4271" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3 \\sqrt{3} \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "d90d1bc8-5ffd-55f7-bcef-454797c5a4db" :answer/id #uuid "d90d1bc8-5ffd-55f7-bcef-454797c5a4db" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12 \\sqrt{3} \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "a7f52bae-425e-5b13-a313-db368e2739f4" :answer/id #uuid "a7f52bae-425e-5b13-a313-db368e2739f4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "36 \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "315f5b77-c3b2-50de-ab4c-837f464ef698" :answer/id #uuid "315f5b77-c3b2-50de-ab4c-837f464ef698" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12 \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "7f9e3b15-fafe-5f5f-9850-3350e5bcb68b" :answer-field/id #uuid "7f9e3b15-fafe-5f5f-9850-3350e5bcb68b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["b570d8ce-b35e-58a9-a28f-8963dd0ebe8a" "a55d1153-0d00-5bef-9de8-4db03c5e4271" "d90d1bc8-5ffd-55f7-bcef-454797c5a4db" "a7f52bae-425e-5b13-a313-db368e2739f4" "315f5b77-c3b2-50de-ab4c-837f464ef698"] :answer-field/correct "d90d1bc8-5ffd-55f7-bcef-454797c5a4db" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "a43799a6-bf36-5f02-8250-1856789bacb5" :question/id #uuid "a43799a6-bf36-5f02-8250-1856789bacb5" :question/problem "![](images/73/73d9ffd3c1ab9ad243b1944fe53a2654f89418f34262e8fd44dcf0ef057e79e5.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\\iint_S 3\\,\\mathrm{d}S,$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=- x + y$ and whose domain is the disk of radius $2$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Give an exact answer." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\\iint_S f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(- x + y)=-1,$$\n\nand\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- x + y)=1.$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}S & =\\iint_D{f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(3)\\cdot\\sqrt{1+(-1)^2+(1)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(3)\\cdot\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ our domain can be expressed as\n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 2,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le 2 \\pi\\}.$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}A & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{(3)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{3 r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\int_{0}^{2}{3 r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\left[\\frac{3 r^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\left[((6))-((0))\\right]}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{6}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(6 \\sqrt{3})\\left[\\theta\\right]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ & =(6 \\sqrt{3})\\left[((2 \\pi))-((0))\\right] \\\\ & =(6 \\sqrt{3})\\cdot((2 \\pi)-(0)) \\\\ & =12 \\sqrt{3} \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral is $12 \\sqrt{3} \\pi$." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["7f9e3b15-fafe-5f5f-9850-3350e5bcb68b"] :db/ensure :question/validate} {:db/id "78db087d-54a3-5074-9702-51a2dfed6bc4" :answer/id #uuid "78db087d-54a3-5074-9702-51a2dfed6bc4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12 \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "b1d7b1f1-76c5-5f4b-906b-3b95fe95622c" :answer/id #uuid "b1d7b1f1-76c5-5f4b-906b-3b95fe95622c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4 \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "e62e88f6-c5ce-5fda-9962-c7bbdfcc6e0a" :answer/id #uuid "e62e88f6-c5ce-5fda-9962-c7bbdfcc6e0a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4 \\sqrt{3} \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "0af59f24-43d6-5c12-b0e9-e9ef1fc3b437" :answer/id #uuid "0af59f24-43d6-5c12-b0e9-e9ef1fc3b437" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2 \\sqrt{3} \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "a2d9c1d2-25db-5084-ac89-6a69bec1929c" :answer/id #uuid "a2d9c1d2-25db-5084-ac89-6a69bec1929c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{3} \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "65cba47d-19ee-5caf-9052-3301717925d3" :answer-field/id #uuid "65cba47d-19ee-5caf-9052-3301717925d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["78db087d-54a3-5074-9702-51a2dfed6bc4" "b1d7b1f1-76c5-5f4b-906b-3b95fe95622c" "e62e88f6-c5ce-5fda-9962-c7bbdfcc6e0a" "0af59f24-43d6-5c12-b0e9-e9ef1fc3b437" "a2d9c1d2-25db-5084-ac89-6a69bec1929c"] :answer-field/correct "e62e88f6-c5ce-5fda-9962-c7bbdfcc6e0a" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "79638187-bf06-5eeb-9e92-84390aacec40" :question/id #uuid "79638187-bf06-5eeb-9e92-84390aacec40" :question/problem "![](images/73/73d9ffd3c1ab9ad243b1944fe53a2654f89418f34262e8fd44dcf0ef057e79e5.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\\iint_S 1\\,\\mathrm{d}S,$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=x - y$ and whose domain is the disk of radius $2$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Give an exact answer." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\\iint_S f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(x - y)=1,$$\n\nand\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(x - y)=-1.$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S{1}\\,\\mathrm{d}S & =\\iint_D{f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(1)\\cdot\\sqrt{1+(1)^2+(-1)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(1)\\cdot\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{1}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ our domain can be expressed as\n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 2,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le 2 \\pi\\}.$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{1}\\,\\mathrm{d}A & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{(1)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\int_{0}^{2}{r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\left[\\frac{r^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\left[((2))-((0))\\right]}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(2 \\sqrt{3})\\left[\\theta\\right]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ & =(2 \\sqrt{3})\\left[((2 \\pi))-((0))\\right] \\\\ & =(2 \\sqrt{3})\\cdot((2 \\pi)-(0)) \\\\ & =4 \\sqrt{3} \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral is $4 \\sqrt{3} \\pi$." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["65cba47d-19ee-5caf-9052-3301717925d3"] :db/ensure :question/validate} {:db/id "0cc3cc91-3f29-59d8-80b6-0509e2fa9b0c" :answer/id #uuid "0cc3cc91-3f29-59d8-80b6-0509e2fa9b0c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4 \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "2e340e39-8adb-52aa-ac10-fd4448c264f7" :answer/id #uuid "2e340e39-8adb-52aa-ac10-fd4448c264f7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4 \\sqrt{3} \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "e9a86298-3ae4-55b4-9fd8-1c22396d65d1" :answer/id #uuid "e9a86298-3ae4-55b4-9fd8-1c22396d65d1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12 \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "32785a38-9413-5c2f-86f3-80004ad4a202" :answer/id #uuid "32785a38-9413-5c2f-86f3-80004ad4a202" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{3} \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "e8d0ede3-115f-5b05-9411-95243502c7ac" :answer/id #uuid "e8d0ede3-115f-5b05-9411-95243502c7ac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2 \\sqrt{3} \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "77e408e6-0de3-55b6-a2e4-474fb0066fcf" :answer-field/id #uuid "77e408e6-0de3-55b6-a2e4-474fb0066fcf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["0cc3cc91-3f29-59d8-80b6-0509e2fa9b0c" "2e340e39-8adb-52aa-ac10-fd4448c264f7" "e9a86298-3ae4-55b4-9fd8-1c22396d65d1" "32785a38-9413-5c2f-86f3-80004ad4a202" "e8d0ede3-115f-5b05-9411-95243502c7ac"] :answer-field/correct "2e340e39-8adb-52aa-ac10-fd4448c264f7" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "4ef90de0-c8fe-5f06-94ae-fa8b5651011c" :question/id #uuid "4ef90de0-c8fe-5f06-94ae-fa8b5651011c" :question/problem "![](images/fb/fbbb7c260754e6579bedc6e773656ea8defdb0493117f05e0e5f02fab4afb36c.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\\iint_S 4\\,\\mathrm{d}S,$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=- x - y$ and whose domain is the disk of radius $1$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Give an exact answer." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\\iint_S f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(- x - y)=-1,$$\n\nand\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- x - y)=-1.$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}S & =\\iint_D{f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(4)\\cdot\\sqrt{1+(-1)^2+(-1)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(4)\\cdot\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ our domain can be expressed as\n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 1,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le 2 \\pi\\}.$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}A & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{(4)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{4 r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\int_{0}^{1}{4 r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\left[2 r^{2}\\right]_{0}^{1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\left[((2))-((0))\\right]}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(2 \\sqrt{3})\\left[\\theta\\right]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ & =(2 \\sqrt{3})\\left[((2 \\pi))-((0))\\right] \\\\ & =(2 \\sqrt{3})\\cdot((2 \\pi)-(0)) \\\\ & =4 \\sqrt{3} \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral is $4 \\sqrt{3} \\pi$." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["77e408e6-0de3-55b6-a2e4-474fb0066fcf"] :db/ensure :question/validate} {:db/id "2417f5d3-5389-5044-b2a0-418aeb7dac86" :answer/id #uuid "2417f5d3-5389-5044-b2a0-418aeb7dac86" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "0b5106df-61b9-5b24-863c-2ae5e567f591" :answer/id #uuid "0b5106df-61b9-5b24-863c-2ae5e567f591" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{12}" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "aaa96423-8207-5f28-adff-de3f6375c04d" :answer/id #uuid "aaa96423-8207-5f28-adff-de3f6375c04d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "61fb6062-d9a8-5fb9-8a3d-bc7a0f08254a" :answer/id #uuid "61fb6062-d9a8-5fb9-8a3d-bc7a0f08254a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "58f3e230-b70f-57b4-82bf-17794d911a1d" :answer/id #uuid "58f3e230-b70f-57b4-82bf-17794d911a1d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "92fd1ae2-283e-5689-8e46-7ea5679c7ca6" :answer-field/id #uuid "92fd1ae2-283e-5689-8e46-7ea5679c7ca6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["2417f5d3-5389-5044-b2a0-418aeb7dac86" "0b5106df-61b9-5b24-863c-2ae5e567f591" "aaa96423-8207-5f28-adff-de3f6375c04d" "61fb6062-d9a8-5fb9-8a3d-bc7a0f08254a" "58f3e230-b70f-57b4-82bf-17794d911a1d"] :answer-field/correct "61fb6062-d9a8-5fb9-8a3d-bc7a0f08254a" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "2c380e3c-eb26-50bd-a669-b181f14f84c3" :question/id #uuid "2c380e3c-eb26-50bd-a669-b181f14f84c3" :question/problem "![](images/79/79b09a881e6809e097e982b58d81a037d162f6b5820894e172955df1551415ca.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\\iint_S x\\,\\mathrm{d}S,$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=- x + y$ and whose domain is the right half-circle of radius $1$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Give an exact answer." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\\iint_S f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(- x + y)=-1,$$\n\nand\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- x + y)=1.$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}S & =\\iint_D{f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(x)\\cdot\\sqrt{1+(-1)^2+(1)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(x)\\cdot\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ our domain can be expressed as\n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 1,\\ - \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}A & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{((r\\cos\\theta))\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\int_{0}^{1}{r^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}r}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\left[\\frac{r^{3}}{3}\\right]_{0}^{1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\left[((\\frac{1}{3}))-((0))\\right]}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\frac{\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})\\left[\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right]_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\ & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})\\left[((1))-((-1))\\right] \\\\ & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})\\cdot((1)-(-1)) \\\\ & =\\frac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral is $\\frac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{3}$." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["92fd1ae2-283e-5689-8e46-7ea5679c7ca6"] :db/ensure :question/validate} {:db/id "bf504992-846a-53c0-b739-ef1c87c02b87" :answer/id #uuid "bf504992-846a-53c0-b739-ef1c87c02b87" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{8 \\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "22f90bd6-2168-5537-aea8-850bd47c55e5" :answer/id #uuid "22f90bd6-2168-5537-aea8-850bd47c55e5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{64 \\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "c8ff7344-e51a-5da3-99bd-f7f28794aacf" :answer/id #uuid "c8ff7344-e51a-5da3-99bd-f7f28794aacf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "64" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "3b6f9c08-fc7c-5686-90ec-bd5e19971270" :answer/id #uuid "3b6f9c08-fc7c-5686-90ec-bd5e19971270" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{32 \\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "9107f827-2e1b-5ff9-a378-2a6c0345e47a" :answer/id #uuid "9107f827-2e1b-5ff9-a378-2a6c0345e47a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{64}{3}" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "0d783c00-550c-539f-9270-98d59cd31448" :answer-field/id #uuid "0d783c00-550c-539f-9270-98d59cd31448" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["bf504992-846a-53c0-b739-ef1c87c02b87" "22f90bd6-2168-5537-aea8-850bd47c55e5" "c8ff7344-e51a-5da3-99bd-f7f28794aacf" "3b6f9c08-fc7c-5686-90ec-bd5e19971270" "9107f827-2e1b-5ff9-a378-2a6c0345e47a"] :answer-field/correct "22f90bd6-2168-5537-aea8-850bd47c55e5" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "141fe36a-507a-57e3-9528-7652085c824c" :question/id #uuid "141fe36a-507a-57e3-9528-7652085c824c" :question/problem "![](images/01/013cdd1ba73ee79ef51d00b7dc9f48488cee2299eb593accadfbf026981169c0.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\\iint_S 4 x\\,\\mathrm{d}S,$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=- x + y$ and whose domain is the right half-circle of radius $2$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Give an exact answer." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\\iint_S f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(- x + y)=-1,$$\n\nand\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- x + y)=1.$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S{4 x}\\,\\mathrm{d}S & =\\iint_D{f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(4 x)\\cdot\\sqrt{1+(-1)^2+(1)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(4 x)\\cdot\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{4 x}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ our domain can be expressed as\n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 2,\\ - \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{4 x}\\,\\mathrm{d}A & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{(4(r\\cos\\theta))\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{4 r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\int_{0}^{2}{4 r^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}r}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\left[\\frac{4 r^{3}}{3}\\right]_{0}^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\left[((\\frac{32}{3}))-((0))\\right]}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\frac{32 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\frac{32 \\sqrt{3}}{3})\\left[\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right]_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\ & =(\\frac{32 \\sqrt{3}}{3})\\left[((1))-((-1))\\right] \\\\ & =(\\frac{32 \\sqrt{3}}{3})\\cdot((1)-(-1)) \\\\ & =\\frac{64 \\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral is $\\frac{64 \\sqrt{3}}{3}$." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["0d783c00-550c-539f-9270-98d59cd31448"] :db/ensure :question/validate} {:db/id "e291533d-3639-5a90-afba-d6eb272eb268" :answer/id #uuid "e291533d-3639-5a90-afba-d6eb272eb268" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4 \\sqrt{3} \\left(-2 + \\sqrt{2}\\right)" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "38dc5a67-0af1-5b0c-9479-a3e6537981d8" :answer/id #uuid "38dc5a67-0af1-5b0c-9479-a3e6537981d8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "- \\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "fd01d698-3524-577e-b710-3910ff2c0119" :answer/id #uuid "fd01d698-3524-577e-b710-3910ff2c0119" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "- 8 \\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "c282306c-0f8e-565e-a571-f8ea4d9770bf" :answer/id #uuid "c282306c-0f8e-565e-a571-f8ea4d9770bf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-24" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "0c882a41-9fb5-5aa6-bfe4-ddedb1e09450" :answer/id #uuid "0c882a41-9fb5-5aa6-bfe4-ddedb1e09450" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-8" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "f56e79b1-49d0-59b4-9498-4712b7f8d4c0" :answer-field/id #uuid "f56e79b1-49d0-59b4-9498-4712b7f8d4c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["e291533d-3639-5a90-afba-d6eb272eb268" "38dc5a67-0af1-5b0c-9479-a3e6537981d8" "fd01d698-3524-577e-b710-3910ff2c0119" "c282306c-0f8e-565e-a571-f8ea4d9770bf" "0c882a41-9fb5-5aa6-bfe4-ddedb1e09450"] :answer-field/correct "fd01d698-3524-577e-b710-3910ff2c0119" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "e3db0b63-f6f4-5b4a-a9c1-b1b2cfd6f6dd" :question/id #uuid "e3db0b63-f6f4-5b4a-a9c1-b1b2cfd6f6dd" :question/problem "![](images/44/44fada7635b47f3f7ef635b0466f21d87c71b52e7f39fddb5d36c16472f4ce82.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\\iint_S 3 x\\,\\mathrm{d}S,$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=- x + y$ and whose domain is the quarter-circle in the second quadrant of radius $2$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Give an exact answer." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\\iint_S f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(- x + y)=-1,$$\n\nand\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- x + y)=1.$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S{3 x}\\,\\mathrm{d}S & =\\iint_D{f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(3 x)\\cdot\\sqrt{1+(-1)^2+(1)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(3 x)\\cdot\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{3 x}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ our domain can be expressed as\n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 2,\\ \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta\\le \\pi\\}.$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{3 x}\\,\\mathrm{d}A & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{(3(r\\cos\\theta))\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{3 r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\int_{0}^{2}{3 r^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}r}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\left[r^{3}\\right]_{0}^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\left[((8))-((0))\\right]}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{8 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(8 \\sqrt{3})\\left[\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right]_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi} \\\\ & =(8 \\sqrt{3})\\left[((0))-((1))\\right] \\\\ & =(8 \\sqrt{3})\\cdot((0)-(1)) \\\\ & =- 8 \\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral is $- 8 \\sqrt{3}$." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["f56e79b1-49d0-59b4-9498-4712b7f8d4c0"] :db/ensure :question/validate} {:db/id "14cdfb2d-5fc7-5150-b284-439e38260d64" :answer/id #uuid "14cdfb2d-5fc7-5150-b284-439e38260d64" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "4676e0a2-75d8-5a17-a969-7c99e3fa2152" :answer/id #uuid "4676e0a2-75d8-5a17-a969-7c99e3fa2152" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "ebbb113c-f187-55e0-8895-4843d66c32f2" :answer/id #uuid "ebbb113c-f187-55e0-8895-4843d66c32f2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{8 \\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "f6db0de2-0b22-5269-b5c3-7a0ea98a8477" :answer/id #uuid "f6db0de2-0b22-5269-b5c3-7a0ea98a8477" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4 \\sqrt{3} \\left(2 - \\sqrt{2}\\right)}{3}" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "7ac954c9-a0ef-5924-b0d7-d5aac29af9a2" :answer/id #